  "synthesis": {
    "description": "When a pipeline or supplier cuts scheduled gas, or a meter is shut in, the scheduler reports the cut to the trading and logistics team with the affected supplier, pipeline point and volumes. The desk then finds replacement supply, for example buying gas from another counterparty to resupply, and reschedules. Some rounds come from the counterparty side, such as a customer claiming force majeure or a supplier announcing it will stop deliveries, and need a summary of the affected days.",
    "steps": [
      "A pipeline, supplier or field contact reports a cut or shut-in.",
      "The scheduler identifies the affected deals, points, gas days and volumes.",
      "The scheduler sends the cut details to the desk and logistics team.",
      "The trader buys replacement gas or adjusts the sale to cover the shortfall.",
      "Nominations are rescheduled with the pipeline and affected parties are informed.",
      "Force majeure claims or supplier notices are summarised for follow-up."
    ],
    "approach": "Feed pipeline cut notices into a standard cut report that looks up the affected deals by point and gas day from the scheduling system, lists the lost volumes, and sends the result to the desk using one message layout. Replacement purchases and rescheduling stay with the trader and scheduler.",
    "rationale": "The cut messages carry the same facts each time: supplier, pipeline point, gas day, volume and replacement. A fixed message layout makes them faster to write and read, and a deal lookup removes the manual search; the buying decision still needs a person.",
    "artifact_type": "email_template"
  },
  "formula": {
